**Prettier 3.9.9**
[Playground link](https://prettier.io/playground/#N4Igxg9gdgLgprEAucAbAhgZ0wAgII7AA6UOOkUmMATgK5gwTUAUJZZADtQJYBu68HOhwBeHK1LsyAemnk2UnAEYFZAJQAaBWsIBfEvqggNICBxjdomZKA7pqmONWQgYATw5xMYHuZC7dIA)

```sh
--parser typescript
```

**Input:**
<!-- prettier-ignore -->
```tsx
class A {
  constructor(
    private a = (
      // c
      1
    ),
  ) {}
}
```

**Output:**
<!-- prettier-ignore -->
```tsx
class A {
  constructor(
    private // c
    a = 1,
  ) {}
}
```

**Second format** (of the output above) **fails:**
```
',' expected. (4:5)
```

**Expected output:**
<!-- prettier-ignore -->
```tsx
class A {
  constructor(
    // c
    private a = 1,
  ) {}
}
```

**Why?**

A line comment between `private` and `a` doesn't parse. For a regular parameter (`function f(a = (// c` + `1)) {}`), Prettier prints the comment before the parameter; the parameter property should do the same.

Related: #16065 (a decorator comment in the same position, fixed).

Also reproduces on `main` (88d8e96).

