**Prettier 3.9.10**
[Playground link](https://prettier.io/playground/#-0BILBx0BMIA5sgYAxKsiQEiiDVmaz2XBmsc4AwalEPoVBQCYBAGI8IMIamdoASEG0fvyJSKyB8ACpC6jCuBvBF1JGHeVQBl1OAARUqEHg8MR7LRwmaykZVtt9rhSGNppAAEc7fBHndjm8QFRhDxYe12j8WVR6oyAMKRKgEj51H7my1wACCMBZNV+lRDQuhsMdJvZdBgRDq2LoCsNdLAcGUrwVNVJCvEBLAwkuh1JbIAklA2mRlGBhad85PlDBxFbq-6iXK4LiqPiUETDULyWrJ4VUJWfSA6qgfjCSTBQ1QGUQM76nYc6QwSQThIPh4ThbA2I1G0MB0MgAAcAAMhxMEGNRMA+T4vn67JiL8QEgWBSAAEyHJUSLalQvxGm+IBwEQvztFObSQlQFqVI+cAAGLSs+RaMpmZYQCAGgaEAA)

```sh
--parser scss
```

**Input:**

```scss
foo, // comment
a[href] {
    --comment-stays: ✅ YES;
}

foo, // comment
a[href=""] {
    --comment-stays: ✅ YES;
}

a[href=""], // comment
foo a[href=""] {
    --comment-stays: ❌ NO /* (until fixed) */;
}

a[href=''], // comment
foo a[href=""] {
    --comment-stays:  ✅ YES;
}
```

**Output:**

```scss
foo, // comment
a[href] {
  --comment-stays: ✅ YES;
}

foo, // comment
a[href=""] {
  --comment-stays: ✅ YES;
}

a[href=""],
// comment foo a[href=""] {
  --comment-stays: ❌ NO /* (until fixed) */;
}

a[href=''], // comment
foo a[href=""] {
  --comment-stays: ✅ YES;
}
```

**Expected output:**

```scss
foo, // comment
a[href] {
  --comment-stays: ✅ YES;
}

foo, // comment
a[href=""] {
  --comment-stays: ✅ YES;
}

a[href=""], // comment
foo a[href=""] {
  --comment-stays: ❌ NO /* (until fixed) */;
}

a[href=''], // comment
foo a[href=""] {
  --comment-stays: ✅ YES;
}
```

**Why?**

The third rule's output changes the code: `foo a[href=""]` is moved into the `//` comment, so that selector is lost. With longer comments the comment's words are each printed on their own line as if they were selectors. 3.6.2 prints the expected output above; 3.7.4 and later (through 3.9.5) do not.

Cause: #17938 changed the comment check in `src/language-css/parse/parse-selector.js` to strip quoted strings before looking for `//` or `/*`:

```js
if (/\/[/*]/.test(selector.replaceAll(/"[^"]+"|'[^']+'/g, ""))) {
```

`"[^"]+"` doesn't match an empty string (`""`). In the third rule the first `"` of `[href=""]` fails to match, so the second `"` pairs with the next selector's opening quote, and the stripped span `"], // comment\nfoo a[href="` takes the comment with it. Prettier then doesn't detect the comment and parses its text as selectors. A comment is only lost when an empty `""` or `''` comes before it and another quote of the same kind comes after it, which is why the other three rules are fine.

Suggested fix: allow empty strings.

```js
if (/\/[/*]/.test(selector.replaceAll(/"[^"]*"|'[^']*'/g, ""))) {
```

I applied this change to the 3.9.5 `plugins/postcss.js` bundle locally. The input above then formats to the expected output, and the #7850 case (`a[href="http://example.com"]`) still formats correctly.

