```python
import pluggy

hookspec = pluggy.HookspecMarker("example")
hookimpl = pluggy.HookimplMarker("example")

class Spec:
    @hookspec
    def hello(self, value):
        pass

class Plugin:
    @hookimpl
    def hello(self, value="fallback", /):
        return value

pm = pluggy.PluginManager("example")
pm.add_hookspecs(Spec)
pm.register(Plugin())
print(pm.hook.hello(value="caller"))
```

Expected: `["caller"]`, following the explicit-value precedence introduced in #738. Actual: `TypeError: ... got some positional-only arguments passed as keyword arguments: 'value'`.

The same failure occurs in new- and old-style wrappers. Removing the default makes positional-only delivery work. If only a later optional positional-only argument is supplied, the earlier argument must retain its implementation default rather than shifting the supplied value.

Reproduced on native Windows and Linux CPython 3.12. The unmodified full suite passes 219 tests on both platforms; a focused added matrix has 13 failures and 9 passing controls on each. This concerns positional-only arguments, not the keyword-only API design in #742. I have a small fix and regression tests prepared.

